2.1.0
docs/dump-hierarchy.md:21:`grantiva hierarchy` finds the session that `--keep-alive` published, opens an HTTP read against the running GrantivaAgent on its allocated port, and prints the XCUI accessibility tree. The session lives in `/tmp/grantiva-sessions/`: grantiva-runner writes `<pid>-<timestamp>.grantiva` (port and session ID) when it starts holding the session, and grantiva writes `<pid>.owner.json` next to it the moment the runner is spawned, recording which simulator UDID (or adb serial) that runner owns, plus `projectDirectory` (the directory the run started in, absolute, with symlinks resolved as realpath(3) resolves them: a run started in `/tmp/x` records `/private/tmp/x`), `platform` (`ios` or `android`), and `detached` (`true` only for `runner start --detach`). The MCP server uses a keep-alive session only when both match its own project directory and platform; both sides are canonicalised the same way, so `/tmp/x` and `/private/tmp/x` match (sidecars written by 2.1.0 with the `/tmp` spelling still match), while other differences, including case, do not. Both files are removed when the run ends; a file whose runner `pid` is no longer running is ignored, and so is a `run --keep-alive` session whose grantiva process is gone, so a crashed or `kill -9`'d run never masquerades as a live session. Its runner does not outlive it either: grantiva binds the runner's process group to its own lifetime, so when the grantiva process dies, however it dies, the runner is interrupted and releases the device within seconds. A `runner start --detach` session is the exception by design: it outlives the command that started it until `grantiva runner stop`.
docs/superpowers/plans/v2-swift-native-runner.md:18:This session alone went through that loop four times (v0.9.0 runner upgrade, v1.0.0 rebrand, v1.0.1 runner-version-mismatch fix, v1.1.0 keep-alive + hierarchy). Each round is 10–20 minutes of mechanical work and adds delta to a patch that has to be re-applied every time the Go runner's base is updated.
docs/superpowers/plans/v2-swift-native-runner.md:81:WDA source stays at `cli/Sources/GrantivaCore/Resources/WebDriverAgent/` pinned to the upstream Facebook fork version we currently ship. We apply minor xcconfig tweaks (display name rebrand from v1.0.0) directly to that checked-in copy — no patch file needed because there's no separate runner codebase to rebase against.
docs/superpowers/plans/v2-swift-native-runner.md:117:- **xctestrun plist injection is fragile.** Format changes between Xcode versions broke the Go runner at least once. *Mitigation:* keep a test fixture per Xcode version; fail fast with a clear message on format mismatch.
docs/superpowers/plans/v2-swift-native-runner.md:132:- **WDA source checkout vs downloaded at install time.** Today we bundle pre-built WDA in the tarball so users don't wait for the first xcodebuild. If the Swift runner only bundles WDA source (no pre-build), the first `grantiva run` on a new machine takes an extra 2–3 minutes while WDA compiles. Do we accept that, or do we ship a pre-built WDA bundle per-Xcode-version as an SPM resource?
README.md:98:- **Concurrent runs** — Runs on different simulator UDIDs execute in parallel. Each simulator launches WebDriverAgent from its own runner home (`<runner home>/versions/<stamp>/devices/<udid>`, where the runner home is `GRANTIVA_RUNNER_HOME` or `~/.grantiva/runner` and `<stamp>` is the installed runner's stamp: the runner version plus the bundled Android driver tag, such as `1.1.28-grantiva.15+android-drivers-2`), which holds an APFS clone of the cached WDA build, so every run gets its own xctestrun, port, and DerivedData without rebuilding WDA. `simulator teardown` (with or without `--force`) and `simulator delete` remove the device's home under every installed `versions/<stamp>/`, and teardown exits non-zero naming any home it could not remove; homes of simulators that no longer exist are pruned on the next run. A second run targeting an already-owned simulator fails immediately with guidance to provision a unique simulator, protecting the active WDA session from cross-run teardown; when the owner is a `grantiva runner start` session the message says so and names `grantiva runner stop`. A run's lease file (`<runner home>/locks/<udid>.lock`) is removed when the run ends, and a deleted simulator's when it is deleted.
README.md:196:| `GRANTIVA_RUNNER_HOME` | Runner directory, default `~/.grantiva/runner`. It holds simulator `locks/`, the WebDriverAgent build `cache/`, and one runner install per version under `versions/<stamp>/`; a home other than the default also holds its own `simulator-capacity/` (see Simulators Grantiva created). When an install is needed (no install of this version yet), grantiva first checks the `grantiva_GrantivaCore.bundle` beside the binary: a bundle from another release fails the install, names the bundle and either both runner versions or the tarball it lacks, leaves any existing install untouched, and leaves no empty `versions/<stamp>/` directory. An existing install of the current version is used as it is, without reading the bundle; `grantiva runner install` then warns when the bundle does not carry that runner, and every command that uses the runner warns (once) when no bundle is beside the binary at all, since the next fresh install would fail. A relative path is resolved against the current directory, and the home is always printed in its resolved form (`/private/tmp/…`, not `/tmp/…`), whether or not it exists yet. |
